        <mxCell id="100" parent="1" vertex="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Availability tests: what they probe and why&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Probes:&lt;/b&gt; https://&amp;lt;public host&amp;gt;/ (the home page). The public host is the Front Door endpoint, or the Static Web App default host in dev (no Front Door). Each custom domain with monitor = true gets its own test.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why the home page:&lt;/b&gt; the site needs no sign-in, so the real page visitors open can be tested directly. No special health page is needed.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;How:&lt;/b&gt; Application Insights Standard web tests from 5 Azure locations (Amsterdam, Dublin, Moscow, Virginia, Singapore), every 5 minutes, 30 s timeout, one retry, GET following redirects.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Passes when:&lt;/b&gt; HTTP 200, and a valid TLS certificate with more than 14 days left.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Path tested:&lt;/b&gt; DNS → WAF → Front Door → Static Web App, the same path visitors take. In dev: straight to the Static Web App.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Alert:&lt;/b&gt; severity 1 when availability across the locations drops below 90% over 5 minutes. Results also feed the workbook dashboard.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Does not prove:&lt;/b&gt; that pages other than the home page load, that the content is correct, or that the Static Web App's default host is locked to Front Door (the origin lock in staticwebapp.config.json)." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;">
          <mxGeometry x="20" y="855.0" width="700" height="260" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="101" parent="1" edge="1" value="" source="100" target="11" style="html=1;edgeStyle=none;endArrow=none;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;strokeColor=#8a8781;strokeWidth=1.2;exitX=0.464;exitY=0;exitPerimeter=0;entryX=0.5;entryY=1;entryPerimeter=0;">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-2" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;How visitors reach the site, and how we know it is up&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;What happens&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;• A visitor opens the site&amp;#x27;s address; Azure Front Door serves it from a nearby edge location and caches the pages&lt;br&gt;• Its firewall blocks clients that send too many requests, and whole countries if configured, before anything reaches the site&lt;br&gt;• Every 5 minutes, tests in 5 regions open the home page and check the certificate&lt;br&gt;• Problems email or page the team, and a shared dashboard shows how the site is doing&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why we do it this way&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;• The audience is external, so the site must be fast and reachable from anywhere&lt;br&gt;• The site only accepts traffic that came through Front Door, so the firewall cannot be bypassed&lt;br&gt;• No sign-in and no backend means very little to attack or maintain" style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="20.0" y="1135.0" width="700.0" height="244" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-3" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;What to keep in mind&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Origin lock is a pipeline step:&lt;/b&gt; the Static Web App accepts only Front Door traffic once the delivery pipeline writes staticwebapp.config.json (the Front Door ID differs per environment). Skip it and the default azurestaticapps.net host bypasses the WAF.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;WAF tier:&lt;/b&gt; managed rule sets (Default Rule Set, Bot Manager) need Front Door Premium, so qa on Standard gets only the rate-limit and geo rules. Start a new WAF in Detection and switch to Prevention after reviewing the blocks.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Dev has no Front Door:&lt;/b&gt; visitors and tests go straight to the Static Web App, so dev shows no WAF or edge behaviour.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Regions:&lt;/b&gt; Static Web Apps runs only in westus2, centralus, eastus2, westeurope and eastasia. Front Door is global.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Custom domains:&lt;/b&gt; the DNS records are created by hand at the DNS provider. Set monitor = true only once DNS points at Front Door.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Region and zones:&lt;/b&gt; the region is the location setting, one of the 5 Static Web Apps regions. Nothing here is zonal: Front Door and the site content are global, and the Terraform state storage is zone- and geo-redundant (GZRS).&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Cost:&lt;/b&gt; about $29 (dev), $73 (qa) and $368 (uat, prod) a month before traffic, at West Europe list prices. Front Door Premium ($330) is 90 % of it, and each monitored custom domain adds a $28 web test. The uat budget (400) is tight." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="20.0" y="1399.0" width="700.0" height="314" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note-4" parent="1" value="&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;Why pick this design, and its trade-offs&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Static Web App, not App Service:&lt;/b&gt; the site has no backend, so a managed host with free TLS, global content delivery and pull-request previews does the job with no server to patch. Trade-off: no server-side code, and only 5 regions.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Front Door in front, not the Static Web App alone:&lt;/b&gt; one place for the WAF, rate limiting, geo blocking, edge caching, custom domains and security headers. Trade-off: Front Door Premium is the largest cost line.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Premium in uat and prod, Standard in qa, none in dev:&lt;/b&gt; managed WAF rules where real traffic arrives, lower cost where it does not. Trade-off: qa and dev cannot rehearse prod&amp;#x27;s WAF or edge behaviour.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Origin lock, not a private endpoint:&lt;/b&gt; the X-Azure-FDID header and the AzureFrontDoor.Backend range stop direct access without a VNet or private networking to pay for. Trade-off: a header and IP-range check, not network isolation, and it depends on the pipeline writing the config.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;No sign-in:&lt;/b&gt; the content is public, so there is nothing to protect behind a login and no identity setup to maintain. Trade-off: nothing on the site can ever be private.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Azure&amp;#x27;s internet web tests, not a probe of our own:&lt;/b&gt; the site is public, so built-in tests from 5 regions follow the real visitor path and warn 14 days before the certificate expires. Trade-off: only the home page and monitored domains are tested.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Monitoring in every environment, forced on in prod:&lt;/b&gt; problems show up in dev too; the switch exists only to save cost outside prod. Trade-off: monitoring costs apply in dev too." style="rounded=1;absoluteArcSize=1;arcSize=12;html=1;fillColor=#f7f7ff;strokeColor=#b9b9e6;dashed=1;dashPattern=4 4;fontColor=#1c1c1c;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry as="geometry" x="20.0" y="1733.0" width="700.0" height="314" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>